# Code Quality

- Use strict TypeScript.
- Prefer discriminated unions over magic strings.
- Prefer readonly data where mutation is not required.
- Avoid giant classes/functions.
- Name functions by business intent.
- Avoid boolean parameters when an enum/domain type is clearer.
- Avoid generic `utils.ts` dumping grounds.
- Avoid circular dependencies.
- Keep dependency injection explicit.
- Validate assumptions at boundaries.
- Fail closed.
- Avoid catch-and-ignore.
- Never swallow errors without a documented reason.
- Preserve original error causes when wrapping errors.
- Use exhaustive handling for domain state machines.
- Prefer composition over inheritance.
- Comments should explain why, not restate what.
- Delete dead code rather than leaving commented-out implementations.
